namespace LeanFM.LLMGenerated.McpLogicImplementation
open LeanFM.ChatUI.Implementation
open LeanFM.LLMGenerated.SelfHosting

def decisions : List ImplementationDecision :=
  [ decision "logic-formula-ast"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reference", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "proof:Submitted logic never runs as code"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "Add a pure module LeanFM.Logic with a closed inductive Formula: true, false, at(state), after(message), terminal, field(message, name, value), predicate(text), derived(name), not, and, or, implies, stronglyImplies, outcomes, follows, leadsTo, precedes, never, EX, AX, EF, AF, EG, AG, EU, AU, EW, AW, and an unsupported(operator) node for K, O, P, Pr and similar modal operators. Parse it from Lean.Json only; no constructor holds code, a path or a closure, so a submitted formula cannot run. Reject bare path operators X, F, G and U as typing errors naming the quantified alternatives. Bound input: 256 formula nodes, depth 64, 256-character strings and 64 KiB of arguments; exceeding an input bound yields LogicInputRejected with the bound named."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: parse every operator; reject code-shaped strings as atoms only, unknown keys, bare F/G/X/U, depth/node/string overflow; no IO in LeanFM.Logic (checked by the module having no IO definitions)"]
  , decision "logic-functions"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.functions"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/CTL.lean"]
      "Formula gains outcomes (fs), follows (a) (bs), leadsTo (a b) and precedes (a b) beside stronglyImplies, and Formula.expand gives each its meaning in the core operators: outcomes as and [EF a, .., AF (or ..)]; follows as AG (implies a (and [EX b, .., AX (or ..)])); leadsTo as stronglyImplies a (AF b); precedes as not (EU (not a) b). Parsed from JSON and from the s-expression under their own names, each refused below its arity (outcomes 2, follows 2, leadsTo and precedes exactly 2); toJson, symbols (the call), reading (its own sentence), subformulas and size over every argument; negate and mode are the expansion's (outcomes: or of AG (negate a) and EG (and of negate a)). Dag.shape keeps each as its own operator, so the s-expression writer prints the call; the DAG compiler and satTree evaluate the expansion. evidence of a function is its expansion's, except that a failed outcomes names the first argument not EF (kind unreachable) before the AF (or ..) loop. The notation writes EU, AU, EW and AW infix (a EU b), their operands parenthesized when binary, and symbolsArg parenthesizes them as operands. CTL.outcomes, CTL.follows, CTL.leadsTo and CTL.precedes are the same expansions over Lean's CTL."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: each function reads the same from JSON and from an s-expression and is refused below its arity; notation, reading and negation; sexpr writes each back as the call and it reads back to the same formula; over a draft with a choice of two ends outcomes is proved while the disjunction of AFs is disproved, an unreachable outcome is named, a path reaching none is a loop; follows of a typestate's next steps is proved and with one next step missing disproved; leadsTo is proved where AG implies is, and disproved, vacuous, when a cannot happen; precedes is proved for an order the draft keeps and disproved with the path where b comes first; the notation of EU, AU, EW and AW is infix", "LeanFM/SessionToolsTests.lean: over PlaceOrder, outcomes of its three ends, follows CreateOrder Quote SoldOut, leadsTo Pay Receipt-or-PaymentDeclined and precedes Quote Pay are proved; the disjunction of AFs is disproved"]
  , decision "logic-words"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.words"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/CTL.lean"]
      "Logic.Word enumerates possibly, inevitably, always, someAlways, allNext, someNext, allUntil, someUntil, allUnless and someUnless, with Word.name, Word.arity (1, or 2 for the until and unless words) and Word.core, the primitive's operator name (EF, AF, AG, EG, AX, EX, AU, EU, AW, EW); Word.ofName? looks a name up. Formula gains one constructor word (w) (fs), and Formula.expand gives word w fs its primitive; the readers build the primitive itself (Word.apply), since LeanFM writes every primitive as its word, so a formula round-trips exactly (word possibly [f] is ef f, allUntil [a, b] is au a b, and so on). Both readers take a word name with exactly its arity and refuse any other count naming the word. toJson writes {name: f} or {name: [a, b]}; symbols writes the word then its operand (inevitably Reply) and the two-place words infix (a allUntil b), and symbolsArg parenthesizes them as operands; reading, negate, mode and evidence go through expand; size and subformulas run over the operands. Dag.shape keeps the word as its own operator so the s-expression writer prints it back; Dag.readOp, Dag.readClauses and the DAG-to-Core compiler look the primitive up with Word.core. Formula.unfold expands every word and function recursively, so explainFormula adds unfolded {sexpr, notation} for the unfolded formula. Printing in words: Formula.symbols writes each primitive as its word (ag f as always f, eu a b as a someUntil b), and Dag.toSexpr and toSexprPretty write each primitive node with Word.ofCore, the word whose core it is; the DAG, the checker and the readings keep the primitives. Formula.coreSymbols and the sexpr writers with core := true write the primitives, for unfolded only. CTL.possibly and the others are the same over Lean's CTL."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: each word reads the same from JSON and from an s-expression, is refused with a wrong count, is written back as written and reads back to the same formula; its notation (inevitably f, a allUntil b) and its reading and negation are the primitive's; over a draft each word gets its primitive's verdict; unfold of a formula mixing words and functions has no word or function left and is decided the same; explain_formula answers unfolded", "LeanFM/SessionToolsTests.lean: over PlaceOrder, always (implies (on CreateOrder) (inevitably ...)) is proved and allNext (on Quote) at the start disproved"]
  , decision "logic-on"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.on"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Suggest.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Instances.lean", "LeanFM/Session/SpecCheck.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "Formula.after and Formula.terminal keep their constructors; toJson writes {on: M} and \"stop\", the s-expression writer (on M) and stop, symbols writes stop, the classifier's atom text is on M and stop, and the readings are M was just delivered and the conversation may stop. The JSON reader takes the key on and the string stop, the s-expression reader the head on and the bare name stop; after and terminal in either form are refused with retiredAtom, naming on or stop. Session.Suggest, Session.SpecCheck and Session.Instances build and read on and stop; the session view's property builder writes them. The logic reference, the MCP descriptions and instructions, the examples, the book generators and the book use on and stop."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: (on A) and stop read in both forms and are written back as written; (after A), terminal, {after: A} and \"terminal\" are refused naming on and stop; toJson writes on and stop", "LeanFM/SessionToolsTests.lean: faithful rules and suggestions print (on ...) and stop and never after or terminal"]
  , decision "logic-state"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.state"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Faithful.lean"]
      "Formula gains state (m) (mayStop); Formula.expand gives and [after m, terminal or not terminal]. It is an atom of the DAG (Dag.shape's default), evaluated by satTree through its expansion and written by atomText as (state M) or (state M stop); symbols writes state M and state M stop; the reading says the conversation is just after M and must go on, or may stop. The s-expression reader takes (state M) and (state M stop) and refuses any other word after the name; the JSON reader takes {state: M} and {state: [M, stop]}. unknownAtoms checks its message as it checks on. Faithful.labelFormula names a message's label with state, the start with no message yet as before, and Faithful.rules writes each typestate's rule as follows (its name) (the names of the typestates that may follow)."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: (state A) and (state A stop) read the same in both forms, are written back as written, and decide as on A with not stop or stop; (state A go) is refused", "LeanFM/SessionToolsTests.lean: PlaceOrder's faithful rules are follows over state terms, each unfolds to always (implies name (and someNext ... allNext (or ...))), all are proved, and the faithfulness verdicts of the Images examples are unchanged"]
  , decision "logic-ctl-model"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "Build a finite Kripke structure from a canonical task (looked up by id in the self-hosting spec) or an inline draft {states, initial, terminals, transitions[src,dst,message]}. A node is (task state, message just taken). Terminal states and deadlocked non-terminal states stutter on themselves so every path is infinite; deadlocks are reported. Evaluate by explicit fixpoint labelling over at most 4096 nodes; iteration is bounded by the node count so every check terminates. Verdicts: proved/disproved only when every atom is observable and the model fit the bound; unknown when a derived or predicate atom is present; unsupported when an unsupported operator is present; resource-limit when the model exceeds the node bound. Unknown state or message names are input errors, so typos are not silently false. A draft transition may carry secrets (secret field paths) and its sender and receiver; a node reached by it keeps them (Node.sender, receiver, secrets, part of nodeKey), and Formula.secret from to field holds at a node whose secrets has a path equal to field or ending in .field (any path when field is absent) and whose sender and receiver match when given; unknownAtoms rejects a secret atom when no transition carries a secret, when no transition carries its field, or when its sender or receiver is no role of any transition, naming which; a role that never handles a secret is answered. Formula.symbols prints the parsed formula in CTL symbols (Formula.symbolsArg parenthesizes only and, or, implies and strongly-implies operands); checkCtl puts it in every answer's common fields and explainFormula beside the reading, so session_ctl, which returns checkCtl's answer, carries it too."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: AG/AF/EG/EF/EU/AU/EW/AW agree with LeanFM.CTL.holds on the self-hosting tasks; notation of AG (Pay -> AF Receipt), not (not A EU B) as ¬(¬A EU B), a AW b as @s AW stop and nested and/or matches the expected symbols and check_ctl carries it; a 5000-state draft yields resource-limit not proved; predicate atom yields unknown; K yields unsupported; a draft with a secret-carrying transition proves EF secret, disproves AG not secret with that step, distinguishes the atom by from, to and field, proves no secret reaches a role that never receives one, prints secret atoms in the notation, and rejects a secret atom over a draft with no secrets, with an absent field or with an unknown role"]
  , decision "logic-protocol"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.protocol", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Attack.lean"]
      "Patterns are LeanFM.Session.Attack.Pat parsed from JSON: a term, {var: name}, or pair, tuple, enc {key, body}, hash, pk and sk over patterns (pk and sk also take a plain name). Steps are {choose: {var, values}}, {fresh: var}, {send: {to, message}}, {recv: pattern} and {claim: {name, args}}; a role may declare sessions, 1 to 4, expanded by Attack.Role.sessions into threads named name, name#2, ... that each run the role once with their own bindings; at most 8 threads of 32 steps, value lists within 64 terms, patterns within 256 nodes. A send or claim whose variables no earlier choose, fresh or recv of its role binds is rejected, naming the role, step and variable. fresh x binds x to the atom x in session 1 and x#k in session k (Attack.freshName); role names and fresh variables may not contain #, no two roles make the same fresh variable, and a fresh value the attacker can already derive is rejected, so fresh values are distinct across sessions and new to the attacker. Attack.coverFresh adds to every domain that holds a fresh value its copies in the other sessions. An optional defender is Attack.Inline {name, protects, knows, drop, rewrite}: a receive by a protected thread (a declared role or its later sessions) takes any message of the expected shape or of a rewrite rule's match that the attacker can build, passes it through Inline.pass, and accepts the result if it matches the receive pattern (Pat.matchMsg). Inline.pass drops when a drop pattern matches, by Pat.matchSeen, any of visibleParts (pairs split, ciphertexts opened only when the defender can build the opening key from analz of its knows; a ciphertext it cannot open matches nothing, since encryption is randomised in practice); otherwise it rewrites a whole message matching a rewrite rule; otherwise it forwards. The delivery is a recv hop from the defender, and its draft transition carries no term, so the attacker learns nothing from it. Protocol.possibleClaims declares on the unreachable flags state every claim a claim step could make over its variables' candidate values, so a formula may name an unreachable claim while a claim no thread could make stays unknown. A defender named like a role or the attacker, protecting an undeclared role, or with a rewrite whose replacement uses a variable its match does not bind is rejected. Attack.Protocol.explore generates the model breadth-first up to 4096 states and marks it truncated if any state was left out; a truncated model answers resource-limit. Otherwise Protocol.draft writes it as a check_ctl draft (every message transition with from, to and term, every claim transition with its claim, the attacker's knowledge declared with seesAll) and check_ctl decides the formula; the answer keeps check_ctl's verdict, reading, mode and evidence, and adds the state and transition counts, the messages on the evidence path (Protocol.replayWire) as {event: send, from, to, via, term} for an honest send, naming its addressee as to and the attacker it went through as via, and {event: recv, from, to, term} for a delivery from the attacker, so the wire and the evidence name the same sender and addressee, and a note of the bounds. check_ctl gains the claimed(claim) atom: a draft transition may carry a claim; a node also holds the claims made on its path, so it is observable; a claim no transition makes is an unknown name."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: a defender dropping bad stops it in plaintext and passes good; bad sealed under pk(B) evades a defender without the key and is delivered by it; a defender holding sk(B) drops it and still delivers sealed good; a rewriting defender turns bad into good, shown in the evidence; undeclared protected roles, a defender named like the attacker and an inventing rewrite are rejected", "LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: Needham-Schroeder declared as JSON: secrecy and agreement disproved for 1978 with Lowe's messages on the wire, proved with Lowe's fix, an honest run still possible; an unbound variable in a send is rejected; an attacker named like a role is rejected; an unknown claim is rejected. NeedhamSchroeder.lean: the same verdicts from the Lean declaration"]
  , decision "logic-secrets"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.secrets", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.protocol", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "ProtocolTool.secretsOf collects the protocol's terms (pattern literals of every step, choose values, domain and domains values, fresh values) and marks by name every atom or symmetric key whose name, with a session suffix #k removed, ends in _secr. Naming is the only mark: a secrets argument is refused, saying to name each secret with _secr; at most 16 secrets. For each secret check_ctl decides {AG: {not: {knows: {actor: attacker, term}}}} over the same Protocol.draft as the formula: proved is kept, disproved is leaked (with its evidence and Protocol.replayWire), anything else, a truncated model included, is resource-limit. ProtocolTool.protectors walks every send pattern of every declared role: for each enc whose body contains a secret (a literal holding the term, or a variable named _secr, or a variable a choose binds to a secret), the protector is the key pattern's opening key (a literal's Msg.inverse, pk(a) gives sk(a), a variable its own name), reported with the role and step. ProtocolTool.lint reports names of atoms, keys and variables containing passw, secret, token, priv or cred (case-insensitive) that are not secrets. With secrets, the formula is optional; without a formula, the verdict is disproved if any secret leaked, resource-limit if any is resource-limit, proved otherwise; a formula keeps its own verdict and the secrets come beside it. Neither a formula nor a secret is an input error. checkProtocol is checkProtocolWith bounds.maxModelNodes, the bound being a parameter only so the cut-off answer can be tested on a small model."
      ["LeanFM/SecretsTests.lean: a login sending H(passwd_secr) in clear keeps passwd_secr (a hash hides its input); a login whose server accepts the hash, so the client sends it named hpasswd_secr, leaks it in clear with the replay on the wire, and under pk(Server) keeps it and names sk(Server) as the protector; a formula asking knows(Ivo, hpasswd_secr) is answered; a secrets argument is refused, naming _secr as the way to mark; a formula asking knows about an unmarked term is refused; 17 secrets are rejected; a name such as password left unmarked is a warning; no formula and no secret is rejected; a model cut off at the state bound (checkProtocolWith with a bound of 3; the tool's is 4096) reports resource-limit for every secret, never kept", "LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: Needham-Schroeder with nb_secr marked and no formula: leaked in 1978 with the wire shown, and leaked with Lowe's fix too, because Bob may run with Ivo, which is why conditional secrecy stays a formula"]
  , decision "mcp-check-metrics"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.metrics", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety"]
      ["LeanFM/CheckMetrics.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "Server.lean"]
      "LeanFM.CheckMetrics keeps a pure Stats: per (tool, verdict) a call count and duration, states and transitions histograms, per tool bound hits and a secrets histogram. Stats.record takes the tool name, the list of listed tool names, the tool's Except String Json result and the elapsed nanoseconds; the tool label is the name if listed, else other; the verdict label is the result's verdict field if it is in a fixed list (proved, disproved, resource-limit, unknown, unsupported, met, violated, undecided, open, ok, issues, conforms, violates, clean, findings, generated, answered, compatible, incompatible, running, finished, stuck, kept, leaked), else other, and rejected for an error; states and transitions are read only as numbers from the result's states and transitions fields; secrets as the length of a secrets array. Histograms use fixed buckets (seconds: 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 60; states and transitions: 10, 100, 1000, 10000, 100000, 1000000; secrets: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16) and render cumulative le buckets, +Inf, _sum and _count with HELP and TYPE lines. A process-wide IO.Ref (initialize checkStats) holds it. MCP's tools/call measures toolResult with IO.monoNanosNow and modifies the ref after the result is computed, so recording cannot change or fail the answer. Server's /metrics appends Stats.render to the existing static metrics."
      ["LeanFM/CheckMetricsTests.lean: an unlisted tool is other; an unknown verdict is other; an error is rejected; histogram buckets are cumulative and end at +Inf with _count; a recorded check_protocol answer whose roles, terms and secrets have distinctive names leaves none of them in the rendered text; every rendered line is a comment or name{labels} value", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: after a session_sla call over JSON-RPC the metrics count it under tool session_sla and verdict met"]
  , decision "logic-reassembly"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reassembly", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence", "proof:Exhausted search never proves"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Reassembly.lean"]
      "LeanFM.Session.Reassembly: a stream position holds empty, right (the signature's byte) or wrong (another byte); a State is the defender's and the host's buffers plus alerted and delivered. Config.segments lists every (offset, run of right/wrong bytes); Config.step applies a segment to the defender with its policy (first keeps a filled position, last overwrites), sets alerted when the defender's buffer is all right, and applies it to the host unless a blocking defender has alerted; delivered is set when the host's buffer is all right. A blocked or delivered state has no successors. Config.explore is breadth-first with a hash index up to 4096 states, one edge per pair of states, and marks truncation; check_reassembly answers resource-limit when truncated. Config.draft writes a check_ctl draft whose transitions carry the claims alerted(sig) and delivered(sig), splitting a segment that makes both through a midpoint, and declares both claims on an unreachable state. Defaults: signature EVIL, box first, host last, blocking, formula never delivered (blocking) or never (delivered and not alerted) (passive). Signatures longer than 4 characters or containing . are rejected, as are unknown policies and modes. The answer keeps check_ctl's verdict, reading, mode and evidence, and adds the segments of the evidence path as {offset, data} with . for a wrong byte, both policies and a note."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: first/last and last/first are evaded, with the two-segment evasions (a decoy byte then the signature over it; the first byte then a decoy over it); first/first and last/last are proved; passive is evaded; long signatures, a . in the signature, unknown policies and modes are rejected", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: check_reassembly is listed and answers over JSON-RPC with segments"]
  , decision "logic-knows-atom"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.knows", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "A knows atom must ask about a marked term, and the only mark is a name: unknownAtoms rejects a knows atom whose term carries no atom or key name ending in _secr (a session suffix #k removed), naming the term and the _secr name to give it. A formula atom {knows: {actor, term}} names an actor declared in the draft's knowledge field {actor: {initial: [terms], seesAll}}; an undeclared actor is an input error, like an unknown state. A draft transition may carry a term. A model node is (task state, message just taken, each declared actor's knowledge), the knowledge taken apart by Knowledge.learn and kept in one order, so equal knowledge compares equal; a transition's term reaches an actor that is its from or to, or every actor that sees all channels. The atom holds at a node when Knowledge.knows decides the term from that node's knowledge. Path evidence lists what each declared actor holds at each step. Canonical tasks declare no knowledge."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: in a draft of Lowe's attack, never(Bob finished with Alice and the network knows nb) is disproved with a path whose last step shows nb held; with Lowe's message it is proved; one state reached with and without a leak is two nodes, so AG and EF disagree there; an undeclared actor is rejected; a formula without knowledge atoms gives the same verdicts as before"]
  , decision "logic-evidence"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.ctl", "property:Logic answers carry their evidence"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "Return evidence as paths of {state, message} steps. Holding existential claims (EF, EX, EU, EG, EW, stronglyImplies) return a witness: breadth-first shortest path for reachability claims and a lasso (prefix plus loop start) for EG. Failing universal claims (AG, never, AX, AF, AU, AW) return a counterexample computed as the witness of the dual existential. A proved universal claim reports the number of reachable nodes checked. A failing stronglyImplies says whether the antecedent is unreachable (vacuous) or the implication fails."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: counterexample for AG not on(LogicInputRejected) on mcp_logic_consult ends with LogicInputRejected; lasso for AF on a draft cycle; witness for EF terminal"]
  , decision "logic-explain"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.explain"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "explain_formula returns the English reading, the negation pushed through duals, the requirement mode (always, never, eventually, possibly or compound), typing errors, unknown atoms when an optional task is supplied, and lints: AG over implies suggests stronglyImplies; AF AG and AG AF are each read explicitly; an optional English sentence containing always/never/every/must with an existential formula, or can/may/possible with a universal one, is flagged as a likely quantifier mismatch. It also writes the formula back as an s-expression from its DAG (Dag.ofFormula, so equal parts are one node and the text is canonical): sexpr is Dag.toSexpr, and sexprPretty is Dag.toSexprPretty, which threads the same labels in the same order as toSexpr and breaks a node onto lines only when its flat text, with the closing parentheses that end its line (one for each enclosing node it is the last argument of), overflows 72 columns at its indentation. Both are computed only when every atom has an s-expression form (after, at, claimed, terminal, secret, unsupported); otherwise both are null and sexprNote names the atom. readingIndented is Dag.toReadingIndented over the same DAG, for every formula (its atoms are read, not printed as s-expressions): Dag.readFlat gives a node's English on one line, threading labels as flatAt does (a node with two or more parents and not an atom is prefixed (#n) where first read and is the formula marked #n after; a non-atomic part inside a sentence is parenthesized), and toReadingIndented keeps a node on one line when indentation plus that text fits 72 columns, otherwise writes each of the operator's clauses (if: / then:, all of these:, on every path, always:, ...) on its own line with its parts below, two spaces in, list parts bulleted with '- ' and their continuation lines four spaces in. check_ctl's notation for a formula too large for a tree is Dag.toSexprPretty, not toSexpr, and each faithful rule row of session_ctl has sexprPretty beside its one-line formula; CtlBookExample writes its s-expression listings with toSexprPretty. check_ctl adds readingIndented to its common fields when the DAG has at most treeLimit nodes, so session_ctl answers carry it, and each faithful rule row has it beside its notation. No new bound: the text is printed from a formula parseBounded already accepted."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: reading and negation of AG(implies(on M, AF stop)); stronglyImplies lint; sentence/quantifier mismatch lint; sexpr and sexprPretty of an s-expression with extra spaces, of the same formula as JSON and of one with a part written twice give the same text, which reads back to the same formula; a long formula breaks one argument per line within 72 columns and reads back; a last argument that fits only without the closing parentheses after it is broken, so no line passes 72; a quoted name stays quoted; a predicate atom gives null with an sexprNote; readingIndented of a short formula is one line, of a long one an outline within 72 columns naming every atom, of a labelled formula reads a shared part once with (#0) and refers to it after, and check_ctl answers carry it; a one-part and or or is read as its part; session_ctl's faithful rule rows each carry sexprPretty, some of it on several lines"]
  , decision "logic-trace"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.trace"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "check_trace accepts at most 512 envelopes {id, prevIds, causeIds, task, src, dst, message}. Report duplicate ids, dangling references, references to later events (a cause must precede its effect in the submitted order), cycles, src/dst contradicting the canonical message schema, concurrent pairs (unordered under the transitive closure of prev and cause edges, listing at most 64), and per task whether the events in submitted order are accepted by that task's typed transitions from its initial state, or the first rejected event. Return a Mermaid sequenceDiagram and a Mermaid causal flowchart (prev solid, cause dashed) generated from the events."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: valid mcp_logic_consult trace accepted with diagram; duplicate, dangling, forward cause, wrong src and out-of-grammar event each reported; two independent requests reported concurrent"]
  , decision "logic-observability"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.observability", "proof:Hidden predicates never pass as observable"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean"]
      "classify_requirement takes a formula or a list of predicate texts and an optional task. at, after, terminal and field atoms are observable once they name a declared state or message; derived(name) atoms are derived and name the reducer or policy declaration that must exist; predicate text is never observable: it is derived when it mentions a declared message or state (needs a reducer over those events) and implementation-only otherwise, with the advice to move it to Implementation.lean or restate it over events and a proposed event name, actor and fields. check_ctl treats predicate and derived atoms as unknown, never as true or false."
      ["LeanFM/LogicTests.lean: 'cache is consistent' is implementation-only with a proposed event; 'LogicVerdict count' is derived; no predicate text is ever observable; check_ctl with a predicate atom never proves"]
  , decision "logic-mcp-wiring"
      ["clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reference", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.knows", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.protocol", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.reassembly", "clause:selfhost.mcp.logic.safety", "task:mcp_logic_consult", "message:LogicConsultRequested", "message:LogicVerdict", "message:LogicInputRejected", "property:Every consultation ends"]
